A||||4||72vinman||Tinting the tail lights, looking for some tips.||||1||
Z||000000||72vinman||10-13-2001||01:07 PM||||I remember awhile back that someone had tinted the tail lights. I think it was Fierolisa. Has anyone else done this? How did you do it? I have some window tint that I was experimenting with but it does not want to sit cleanly on the lens without wrinkles on the curved ends. Anyone have some ideas? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||10-13-2001||03:21 PM||||First thing is check your local laws... this is not legal in many places.<P>The next thing is make sure you have a second set of tail lights. Once they are tinted it may be imposible to undo it if you went too dark or decide later you hate it.<P>You may also need the second set for DMV inspections. You may get away with tint on the street only to have a DMV inspector fail you for it.<P>You might also want to read thru the lighting site linked in my cave... it in the safety article I think. He covers allot of the stuff happening with lighting issues.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||poupas||10-13-2001||03:46 PM||||I have my taillights tinted. I am not sure if it is illegal here in Canada but I have never had a problem with it. They have been done for almost 6 years. I used transparent taillight paint. I bought it at a aftermarket retailer. I dont know if they still sell it, but it cost about $10 Canadian. All you do is remove your taillight lens, and spray on a few coats of this paint. It is made for this job. It darkens the taillights but still lets the light shine through. Looks good.||||reg||1
Z||000003||72vinman||10-13-2001||05:53 PM||||Thanks for the tip Ogre. I did think about that but i have talked to a friend who is a policeman. He did not think it was a problem if the lights are clearly visible when applied.It can't be too dark. But even if it is I might still take the chance as I find the stock lights on the GT very outdated looking.<P>Poupas, do you remember where you bought it or what it is called. Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000004||qwikgta||10-15-2001||11:06 PM||||I just did up a set of homemade clear lens and I have an Idea for ya, I am going to do this so all I ask is if you do it mention that I gave you the idea.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Take the lens assy apart and remove the clear/red plastic parts.  Get some thin pin-stripe tape from the local Autoparts store.  Take the tape and put it on, on the inside of the lens cover about a half inch apart, it will be a pain in the ass to do but it will pay off.  After the tape is down, get some black paint, gloss/flat/semi up to you and paint the lens cover over the tape you just put down.  After it is dry remove the tape and put the part together again, (leave off the red/clear plastic parts)  Install back into car and use red bulbs (again from auto store).  You will have a black lens and where the tape was you will see the red light shine through.  Get diff size tape to see what size you want to go with and maybe adjust the tape seperation agian for your taste.  I have thought about doing it in the original block pattern too.  <P>Good luck<P>Rob<P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe, 66K, Header no Cat w/ Supertrapp, 16" GTZ rims, short shifter, GT rear swaybar, open element air cleaner, hood vents, clear tails, KYB's, dropped springs.<P>http://www.homestead.com/robsfieroproject/robsfieroproject.html<BR>   <BR>http://www.vafieros.com<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
